        "aiSupplement": {
          "explanation": [
            {
              "text": "In a halogen displacement reaction the more reactive halogen (the stronger oxidising agent) takes electrons from the halide ion of a less reactive halogen. Here Y<sub>2</sub> displaces X<sub>2</sub>, so Y is above X in Group 17: Y = chlorine, X = bromine.",
              "refs": [
                "syllabus:11.2(1)"
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "The X<sup>–</sup> ions in NaX lose electrons (–1 → 0) and reduce Y<sub>2</sub>. A species that gives electrons is a reducing agent, so NaX behaves as a reducing agent.",
              "refs": [
                "syllabus:11.3(1)",
                "syllabus:6.1(4)"
              ]
            }
          ],
          "optionAnalysis": [
            {
              "key": "A",
              "correct": false,
              "text": "The halogens are the right way round, but NaX is oxidised (it loses electrons), so it is the reducing agent, not the oxidising agent."
            },
            {
              "key": "B",
              "correct": true,
              "text": "Chlorine (Y) is the stronger oxidising agent and displaces bromine (X); Br<sup>–</sup> in NaBr gives electrons to Cl<sub>2</sub>, so NaX acts as a reducing agent."
            },
            {
              "key": "C",
              "correct": false,
              "text": "Bromine cannot displace chlorine from chloride ions, and NaX is not the oxidising agent."
            },
            {
              "key": "D",
              "correct": false,
              "text": "NaX is the reducing agent, but bromine cannot oxidise Cl<sup>–</sup> to Cl<sub>2</sub>, so Y cannot be bromine with X as chlorine."
            }
          ],
          "reasoningChain": [
            "Y<sub>2</sub> displaces X<sub>2</sub>, so Y is the more reactive halogen (stronger oxidising agent).",
            "Of chlorine and bromine, chlorine is more reactive: Y = chlorine, X = bromine.",
            "X<sup>–</sup> → ½X<sub>2</sub> + e<sup>–</sup>: NaX loses electrons, so it is a reducing agent.",
            "Option B."
